---
// Mark, tagline headline, one-line outcome-framed subhead, CTA — wrapped in a typed
// boot-sequence. Home is now Hero-only (see SPEC.md's structural-redesign revision
// note); the operator--info line folds in what used to be a separate IdentityHook
// component below the Hero. See the "Stripped-down terminal" note further down for
// why this is no longer the literal CRT-device treatment SPEC.md originally
// described — that description is now stale and BRANDFINAL.md has been updated to
// match what's actually built here.
//
// Headline is the approved tagline verbatim (BRANDFINAL.md — Tagline). Subhead
// (rewritten in the copy pass) leads with the full-lifecycle work itself, deliberately
// not AI-specific — same "not AI-first" framing decision as the home-finalization
// pass, just newer wording. Operator line (also rewritten in the copy pass, to a
// skills-summary framing rather than the years-contracting fact — same "Hoda -"
// lead-in and (more) link to /about kept) still points at the same real bio /about
// tells in full.
//
// Single-frame terminal: everything (boot log, headline, subhead, CTA) lives inside ONE
// left-aligned `.terminal-body`, not a separately bordered/tinted box nested inside the
// outer CRT bezel — a nested box read as "a terminal within a terminal." The
// headline/subhead are the terminal's own biggest lines of output, in the same mono
// font and `>`/`//` prompt conventions as everything above them (BRANDFINAL.md's
// type-system note: the pixel font's Hero-headline use no longer applies to Home). No
// brand-row here — Header already shows the mark+wordmark on every page including Home.
//
// One-time sequence, not a loop: an earlier pass tried making the boot log replay
// forever with the tagline permanently static and independent of it. Live use showed
// that's not wanted — the sequence should play once, end with the tagline as its own
// final output, and the whole thing (log + tagline) stays on screen together
// afterward. Headline/subhead/CTA are timed items on the same `clock` the boot log
// uses (`blockLine()`), revealed via `.line-reveal` same as the operator-response line.
// A `boot-skip` class (set once per session, after the sequence first completes) skips
// straight to the fully "booted" end state on repeat visits to Home, so revisiting
// doesn't mean sitting through the whole thing again with the tagline hidden meanwhile.
//
// Sized to content, not the viewport: a prior pass forced the bezel to fill the full
// viewport height, which just left empty space below the (fixed-length) content —
// reverted to a normal content-sized frame, capped at --content-max-width. That
// failure was specifically about forcing the OUTER FRAME's height to the viewport,
// not about type scale — pushed further in the "bolder, structurally" round by
// growing the headline/CTA/terminal text sizes instead, which carries no risk of
// recreating the empty-space bug since the bezel itself still sizes to content.
//
// Typing: every single-line item (preamble/operator command) types out
// character-by-character via the width-clip `.typed` technique. Anything that wraps
// onto multiple lines (operator-response, headline,
// subhead) does NOT — animating a fixed inline-block's width while its content wraps
// causes the browser to re-wrap the partially-revealed text at every animation step,
// throwing line breaks around chaotically (this broke the operator-response line in an
// earlier pass — see git history). Wrapped content uses `.line-reveal` instead: a
// real eased opacity+translateY transition (not an instant snap — see the CSS comment
// on `.line-reveal` for why a `steps(1)` "instant" version actually never appeared at
// all on first load, a real bug fixed alongside making this reveal smoother/more
// deliberate per live feedback). Keep new multi-line content on `.line-reveal`, not
// `.typed` — standing rule.
//
// Smoothness pass (explicit request - "drastic," same treatment already applied to
// About/Contact): the character-by-character `.typed` boot text and the caret blink
// are NOT touched here — that steps()-timed typing IS Home's actual identity (the
// literal thing CLAUDE.md protects as Hero-exclusive), not a gap to smooth over. What
// WAS a gap: the bezel itself had zero arrival motion (it just appeared, fully
// formed, with typing starting instantly inside it), the status LED sat static while
// every other "system operational" dot on the site pulses, and the CTA snapped into
// view with no distinction from a plain text line. Added: a brief "power-on" for the
// bezel (fade + scale + a glow that flashes brighter then settles, cubic-bezier
// (0.16,1,0.3,1) - the same curve `.line-reveal` below already used, so this isn't a
// new register, just extending one that already existed here), a pulsing LED matching
// Footer/About/Contact's status-dot convention, a smooth hover on the operator line's
// "(more)" link, and the same ambient glow used on About's photo / Contact's
// confirmation now marking the CTA as it arrives - the page's actual conversion
// point. The bezel entrance replays on every visit to Home (like About's photo does
// on every visit to About) - only the boot-skip class is about not re-typing the same
// text you've already read, which is unrelated to whether the frame itself fades in.
//
// Stripped-down terminal, round two (explicit request, authorized to override
// BRANDFINAL.md's existing description of this element - the doc's "console
// buttons"/"scanlines"/corner-rivets description of Home's hero no longer matches
// what's built here and needs updating to match, not the other way around): the
// literal skeuomorphic device chrome - the window-buttons bar, the LED, the
// "xheia_terminal" label, the bezel's border + inset shadow, the scanline texture,
// the corner rivets - is gone. What's left is the actual identity worth keeping:
// mono type, `$`/`>` prompt glyphs, real character-by-character typing, the caret.
// That's a terminal in the sense of "typed text on a prompt," not "a CRT monitor
// prop sitting on the page." Whitespace does the framing job the border used to do.
// The boot text itself is cut down to match - the module-loading flourish
// (cleansing.sys/warehousing.sys/agentic_ai.sys with progress bars) and the
// `$ whoami` exchange are removed entirely; neither carried unique information
// (the module names are decorative restatements of /approach's real Capabilities
// section, and "full-stack data consultant" is restated by the headline/operator
// line anyway) - cutting them is a content simplification, not a content loss.
// PixelRivets.astro is now unused anywhere on the site and has been deleted rather
// than left as dead code.
import CTAButton from './CTAButton.astro';

const CPS = 140; // characters typed per second — fast but still visibly "typed"
const DWELL = 0.04; // pause after a line finishes before the next one starts
const BEAT = 0.1; // longer pause marking a new "section" of the session

let clock = 0;

const typeLine = (glyph: '$' | '>', text: string) => {
  const delay = clock;
  const duration = text.length / CPS;
  clock = delay + duration + DWELL;
  return { glyph, text, delay, duration };
};

// A full character of slack so font-metric rounding can't clip the last glyph — the
// reveal still reads as "typed to completion," just with breathing room at the edge.
const padWidth = (charCount: number) => `${charCount + 1}ch`;

// Same delay+duration budget as typeLine(), for lines that reveal as a block instead
// of typing character-by-character (see the wrapping note above).
const blockLine = (text: string) => {
  const delay = clock;
  const duration = text.length / CPS;
  clock = delay + duration + DWELL;
  return { delay, duration };
};

const preamble = [typeLine('$', 'xheia --init'), typeLine('>', 'system ready')];

clock += BEAT;
const operatorCommand = typeLine('$', 'operator --info');
const operatorResponseText =
  'Hoda - multidisciplinary across BI, automation and data strategy. (more)';
const operatorResponse = blockLine(operatorResponseText);
const finalCaretDelay = operatorResponse.delay + operatorResponse.duration + 0.04;

clock += BEAT;
const headlineText = 'I turn fragmented data into systems you can actually trust.';
const headline = blockLine(headlineText);

const subheadText =
  'From cleansing and migration to warehousing and BI - building the foundation that makes reliable decisions possible.';
const subhead = blockLine(subheadText);

const ctaDelay = clock + 0.04;
const revealDelayMs = Math.ceil(ctaDelay * 1000);
---

<section class="hero">
  <div class="bezel">
    <div class="screen">
      <div class="terminal-body">
        {
          preamble.map((line) => (
            <p class="boot-line">
              <span class="prompt-glyph">{line.glyph}</span>{' '}
              <span
                class="typed"
                style={{
                  width: padWidth(line.text.length),
                  animationDuration: `${line.duration}s`,
                  animationDelay: `${line.delay}s`,
                  animationTimingFunction: `steps(${line.text.length}, end)`,
                }}
              >
                {line.text}
              </span>
            </p>
          ))
        }

        <p class="boot-line">
          <span class="prompt-glyph">{operatorCommand.glyph}</span>{' '}
          <span
            class="typed"
            style={{
              width: padWidth(operatorCommand.text.length),
              animationDuration: `${operatorCommand.duration}s`,
              animationDelay: `${operatorCommand.delay}s`,
              animationTimingFunction: `steps(${operatorCommand.text.length}, end)`,
            }}
          >
            {operatorCommand.text}
          </span>
        </p>
        <p
          class="boot-line prompt-response operator-line line-reveal"
          style={{ animationDelay: `${operatorResponse.delay + operatorResponse.duration}s` }}
        >
          <span class="prompt-glyph">&gt;</span>{' '}
          Hoda - multidisciplinary across BI, automation and data strategy. (<a
            href="/about">more</a
          >)<span
            class="caret"
            style={{ animationDelay: `${finalCaretDelay}s` }}
            aria-hidden="true"
          ></span>
        </p>

        <h1
          class="headline line-reveal"
          style={{ animationDelay: `${headline.delay + headline.duration}s` }}
        >
          <span class="prompt-glyph" aria-hidden="true">&gt;</span> {headlineText}
        </h1>

        <p
          class="subhead line-reveal"
          style={{ animationDelay: `${subhead.delay + subhead.duration}s` }}
        >
          <span class="comment-glyph" aria-hidden="true">//</span> {subheadText}
        </p>

        <div class="cta-wrap line-reveal" style={{ animationDelay: `${ctaDelay}s` }}>
          <CTAButton class="hero-cta" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<script is:inline define:vars={{ revealDelayMs }}>
  // is:inline re-executes this block on every soft navigation that lands on Home, so
  // the addEventListener call itself must be guarded — otherwise each revisit stacks
  // another listener for the session's lifetime (same guard pattern as PixelCursor.astro).
  if (!window.__xheiaBootBound) {
    window.__xheiaBootBound = true;
    document.addEventListener('astro:page-load', () => {
      const bezel = document.querySelector('.hero .bezel');
      if (!bezel) return;
      if (sessionStorage.getItem('xheia-boot-played')) {
        bezel.classList.add('boot-skip');
      } else {
        setTimeout(() => sessionStorage.setItem('xheia-boot-played', '1'), revealDelayMs);
      }
    });
  }
</script>

<style>
  /* A flat black void reads as empty, not spare - real dark-mode product pages
     almost always have a soft light source somewhere on the page rather than a
     uniform black field. One large, very low-opacity radial glow behind the
     content, blending the same two accents already used everywhere else on this
     page (the operator/CTA glow's blue, the caret/status green) - environment
     lighting, not a visible shape. Purely decorative background, inert. */
  .hero {
    position: relative;
    display: flex;
    justify-content: center;
    align-items: center;
    min-height: calc(100vh - 5rem);
    padding: var(--space-8) var(--space-4);
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  /* Spans the whole section (not a smaller fixed-size box centered inside it) so
     each radial-gradient's own fade-to-transparent is what disappears the glow,
     not a hard edge where a smaller box meets the page background - a fixed
     62rem/44rem box here was showing exactly that seam as a visible rectangle
     against light mode's bright background (invisible in dark mode, where the
     seam blended into the darkness, but real). */
  .hero::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    background:
      radial-gradient(
        28% 42% at 32% 38%,
        color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent-primary) 16%, transparent),
        transparent 100%
      ),
      radial-gradient(
        22% 34% at 70% 68%,
        color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent-status) 10%, transparent),
        transparent 100%
      );
    pointer-events: none;
    z-index: 0;
  }

  /* Measured, not eyeballed, after two rounds that still read as "just a white
     page": sampled actual rendered pixels at the glow's peak vs. plain background.
     The previous version (14%/5%) moved the peak to a LOWER luminance than the
     background (~220 vs ~240) - i.e. it was rendering as a faint shadow, not a
     light source, because color-mix toward a saturated blue or toward --text both
     necessarily darken a near-white base. Human contrast sensitivity is far lower
     near white than near black (Weber-Fechner) - the same percentage shift that
     reads as an obvious glow on Ink is nearly imperceptible on Paper. Fixed by
     going meaningfully bolder on the blue (25%) so the tint itself is what
     registers, not a brightness delta - this is closer to how Apple's own light-
     mode marketing pages actually look (a visible soft blue/lavender wash), not a
     token gesture at "some atmosphere." Coral still dropped entirely (see below).
     Peak position measured and moved off the boot-line readout text specifically:
     at 36% vertical it sat almost exactly under that small quiet text, and
     --text-secondary's 5.37:1 (measured against flat Paper) dropped to 4.59:1
     against the tinted area behind it - real but too close to the 4.5:1 minimum to
     leave alone. Shifted down toward the headline instead (which sits on --text at
     full strength, nowhere near its own contrast floor regardless of tint). */
  :global(html[data-theme='light']) .hero::before {
    background:
      radial-gradient(
        34% 50% at 28% 54%,
        color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent-primary) 25%, transparent),
        transparent 100%
      ),
      radial-gradient(
        28% 42% at 74% 78%,
        color-mix(in srgb, var(--text) 6%, transparent),
        transparent 100%
      );
  }

  /* No border, no inset shadow, no window-chrome bar - the literal "device" framing
     is gone (see the top comment block). Whitespace (.screen's generous padding
     below) is what marks this out as its own moment now, the way a bordered panel
     used to. */
  .bezel {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
    width: 100%;
    max-width: var(--content-max-width);
    opacity: 0;
    transform: scale(0.98);
    animation: bezel-power-on 0.6s cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1) forwards;
  }

  @keyframes bezel-power-on {
    to {
      opacity: 1;
      transform: scale(1);
    }
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .bezel {
      opacity: 1;
      transform: none;
      animation: none;
    }
  }

  .screen {
    position: relative;
    padding: var(--space-16) var(--space-8);
  }

  .terminal-body {
    max-width: 46rem;
    margin: 0 auto;
    text-align: left;
  }

  /* Deliberately small and quiet - a system-readout preamble, not body text. The
     headline below is the one thing on this page meant to command attention;
     everything above it should read as a quick status check you glance past, not
     compete with it for weight (Apple HIG "one strong idea per screen" logic). */
  .boot-line {
    position: relative;
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-1);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .prompt-response {
    color: var(--text-secondary);
  }

  .operator-line {
    white-space: normal;
  }

  .operator-line a {
    color: var(--accent-primary);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    text-underline-offset: 0.15em;
    text-decoration-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent-primary) 35%, transparent);
    transition:
      text-decoration-color 0.3s cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1),
      color 0.3s cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1);
  }

  /* Underline-opacity alone isn't a visible keyboard-focus indicator - same fix as
     Header/Footer's nav links. */
  .operator-line a:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--accent-primary);
    outline-offset: 2px;
  }

  .operator-line a:hover,
  .operator-line a:focus-visible {
    text-decoration-color: currentColor;
  }

  .prompt-glyph {
    color: var(--accent-primary);
  }

  .comment-glyph {
    color: var(--text-secondary);
  }

  .typed {
    display: inline-block;
    overflow: hidden;
    vertical-align: bottom;
    width: 0;
    animation-name: type-reveal;
    /* `both`, not just `forwards`: the final width is set inline (it's computed
       per-line from character count), which beats this class's `width: 0` in the
       cascade whenever the animation isn't actively controlling the property -
       i.e. during its own delay. Without `backwards`, every line sat fully
       visible from the start, then snapped invisible and re-grew the instant its
       delay elapsed - a real flash-then-regrow glitch, the opposite of smooth,
       found while verifying this pass. `both` makes the first keyframe (width: 0)
       apply for the whole delay, so lines stay properly hidden until their turn. */
    animation-fill-mode: both;
  }

  @keyframes type-reveal {
    from {
      width: 0;
    }
  }

  .caret {
    display: inline-block;
    width: 0.5em;
    height: 1em;
    margin-left: 0.25em;
    background: var(--accent-status);
    vertical-align: text-bottom;
    opacity: 0;
    animation:
      caret-appear 0s forwards,
      caret-blink 1s steps(1) infinite;
    /* animation-delay set inline per-instance (see markup) — overrides any value here. */
  }

  /* Anything that wraps onto multiple lines (see the frontmatter note on why it can't
     use `.typed`) reveals as a real eased transition instead — one shared class so the
     reduced-motion/boot-skip overrides below only need to say it once.
     NOTE: this used to be an "instant" 0.01s steps(1) snap, which had a real bug — the
     delay values are computed floating-point numbers (e.g. 7.049999999999999s), and
     steps(1) changes value only at the EXACT end of its duration; landing fractionally
     short of that exact instant due to float imprecision meant the animation reported
     as "finished" via the Web Animations API while still rendering its 0%-keyframe
     value, i.e. permanently invisible. A smooth (non-stepped) timing function has no
     such cliff — being 0.0001% short of "done" is visually indistinguishable from done. */
  .line-reveal {
    opacity: 0;
    transform: translateY(0.6em);
    animation: line-reveal-in 0.45s cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1) forwards;
  }

  @keyframes line-reveal-in {
    to {
      opacity: 1;
      transform: translateY(0);
    }
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .caret {
      animation: none;
      opacity: 1;
    }

    .typed {
      width: auto !important;
      animation: none;
    }

    .line-reveal {
      opacity: 1;
      transform: none;
      animation: none;
    }
  }

  /* Session-repeat visits: skip straight to the fully "booted" state instead of
     replaying (and re-hiding the headline/subhead/CTA behind) the whole sequence — see
     the `astro:page-load` script above. Same end state as the reduced-motion block
     above, just gated on a runtime class instead of a media feature. */
  .bezel.boot-skip .typed {
    width: auto !important;
    animation: none;
  }

  .bezel.boot-skip .caret {
    animation: none;
    opacity: 1;
  }

  .bezel.boot-skip .line-reveal {
    opacity: 1;
    transform: none;
    animation: none;
  }

  @keyframes caret-appear {
    to {
      opacity: 1;
    }
  }

  @keyframes caret-blink {
    0%,
    49% {
      opacity: 1;
    }
    50%,
    100% {
      opacity: 0;
    }
  }

  /* The one thing on this page meant to command attention - a real scale jump from
     the quiet readout above it, not an incremental bump. Tight line-height and a
     touch of negative tracking at this size, the way a confident display headline
     reads rather than a stretched-out paragraph. */
  .headline {
    position: relative;
    margin: var(--space-12) 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: clamp(2.25rem, 3.4vw + 1.1rem, 3.75rem);
    line-height: 1.1;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    color: var(--text);
    white-space: normal;
  }

  .subhead {
    position: relative;
    margin: var(--space-6) 0 0;
    max-width: 34rem;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    white-space: normal;
  }

  .cta-wrap {
    /* Shrink to the button's own size, not the full terminal width - otherwise the
       glow below renders as a wide bar spanning the whole row instead of a soft
       halo hugging the button. */
    display: inline-block;
    margin: var(--space-8) 0 0;
  }

  /* The CTA is Home's actual conversion point - marking its arrival with the same
     ambient glow used on About's photo / Contact's confirmation ties this page's
     signature moment to the same visual language, instead of the button just
     appearing like any other line of text. Overrides only animation-name (not the
     shared .line-reveal rule's duration/delay/timing-function), so it still respects
     the inline animation-delay set on this element and stays in sync with the rest
     of the reveal sequence. */
  .cta-wrap.line-reveal {
    animation-name: cta-reveal-in;
  }

  @keyframes cta-reveal-in {
    from {
      opacity: 0;
      transform: translateY(0.6em);
      box-shadow: 0 0 0 0 transparent;
    }
    to {
      opacity: 1;
      transform: translateY(0);
      box-shadow: 0 0 2rem -0.4rem color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent-primary) 55%, transparent);
    }
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .cta-wrap.line-reveal {
      animation: none;
      opacity: 1;
      transform: none;
      box-shadow: none;
    }
  }

  .bezel.boot-skip .cta-wrap.line-reveal {
    animation: none;
    opacity: 1;
    transform: none;
    box-shadow: 0 0 2rem -0.4rem color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent-primary) 55%, transparent);
  }

  /* Bigger than the site's default CTAButton size - the tagline's own "so what,
     do this next" moment deserves more visual weight than a standard inline CTA
     (Apple HIG design-improvement pass, "bolder structurally" round). */
  .cta-wrap :global(.hero-cta) {
    padding: var(--space-3) var(--space-6);
    font-size: 1rem;
  }
</style>
